{"title":"Count Ways to Fill Zeros","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"You are given an integer array `arr` of length `n`. Some positions hold `0`, which means the value there is **unknown**; every other position holds a fixed non-zero integer. At least one element is non-zero.\n\nReplace every `0` with any integer (the replacement may be negative, positive or even `0`, and different zeros may receive different values) so that every pair of adjacent elements differs by **at most one**: `|arr[i] - arr[i + 1]| ≤ 1` for all `i`. Fixed values must not be changed.\n\nCount the number of different valid arrays you can obtain. Two results are different if they differ at some position. Print the count modulo `1000000007`; if no valid filling exists the answer is `0`.","inputFormat":"The first line contains `n`.\nThe second line contains `n` integers: the array `arr`.","outputFormat":"Print a single integer: the number of valid fillings modulo `1000000007`.","constraints":["1 ≤ n ≤ 1500","-10^9 ≤ arr[i] ≤ 10^9","At least one element is non-zero"],"examples":[{"input":"4\n3 0 0 4","output":"6","explanation":"The two zeros sit between the fixed values 3 and 4, so the three steps 3 -> x -> y -> 4 must each be -1, 0 or +1 and add up to +1. The valid fillings (x, y) are (3, 3), (3, 4), (4, 4), (2, 3), (4, 3) and (4, 5): that is one +1 with two 0 steps in 3 orders, plus two +1 steps with one -1 step in 3 orders, so 3 + 3 = 6."},{"input":"4\n0 0 5 0","output":"27","explanation":"A zero run at an end of the array has only one fixed neighbour, so each zero can differ from the previous element by -1, 0 or +1 freely. The two leading zeros give 3 * 3 = 9 fillings and the trailing zero gives 3, for 9 * 3 = 27."},{"input":"5\n2 0 0 0 7","output":"0","explanation":"Going from 2 to 7 needs a rise of 5, but the four steps across the three zeros can rise by at most 4 in total, so no filling exists."}],"tests":[{"input":"1\n5","output":"1"},{"input":"2\n0 3","output":"3"},{"input":"3\n1 0 1","ou